<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso X of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1221">1221</a>–<a href="/years/1284">1284</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Alfonso the Wise</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso X, called the Wise, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León from 1252 to 1284. He suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> revolt of 1264 to 1266 and brought the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> under direct Castilian rule. A great patron of learning, he sponsored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-partidas">Siete Partidas</a> law code, the Alfonsine astronomical tables and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile.jpg" alt="Alfonso X of Castile" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfonso_X_el_Sabio_en_El_libro_de_los_juegos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfonso was born on 23 November 1221 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, the eldest son of Ferdinand III and Elizabeth (Beatrice) of Swabia. His mother was a cousin of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, to whom he is often compared. For his first nine years he was heir only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>; then his father united the kingdoms of Castile and León. He began as a soldier under his father at sixteen, and in the 1240s he helped conquer Muslim strongholds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a> and Cádiz. In 1249 he married Violante, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Alfonso succeeded his father in 1252. He invaded Portugal in 1253 and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, which he returned in 1263. In 1254 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> and renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>'s claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> when his half-sister Eleanor married Henry's son Edward. In 1256 his descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> gave him a claim to the German crown, and a dissident faction elected him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1257. He never went to Germany, while his rival Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>. To raise money he debased the coinage, then set an arbitrary tariff to hold prices down, which ruined what trade there was. His nobles rebelled in 1272, and in 1275 the Pope won his oral renunciation of the imperial claim.</p><p>His eldest son Ferdinand died in 1275 at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecija">Écija</a>, leaving two infant sons. Alfonso's second son Sancho claimed to be heir, with the nobles' support, and a civil war led Alfonso in 1282 to accept Sancho. Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> stayed loyal to the king. He died at Seville in 1284, leaving a will that tried to exclude Sancho and a legacy of civil war. In 1273 he created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesta">Mesta</a>, an association of sheep owners, which later grew powerful.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Alfonso's court employed Jewish, Christian and Muslim scholars who translated works from Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Castilian under his supervision, and Castilian came to replace Latin in the royal chancery. Their main work was in astronomy and astrology, including the Alfonsine tables based on the calculations of al-Zarqali. He sponsored the Crónica general, finished in 1264, and the General Estoria, a world history left incomplete at the birth of Christ. He began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-partidas">Siete Partidas</a>, one of the most comprehensive law codes of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, though it was promulgated only by his great-grandson. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria</a>, in Galician–Portuguese, comprise 420 poems with musical notation, mostly on miracles attributed to the Virgin Mary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfonso X of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their split in 1257 had produced two kings, Richard of Cornwall and <b>Alfonso X of Castile</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Dante's Europe also had substantial contact with the Muslim world, through Averroism and the patronage of <b>Alfonso X of Castile</b>; Frederick Copleston argued in 1950 that Dante's respect for Averroes and Avicenna acknowledges a “considerable debt” to Islamic philosophy.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cantigas de Santa Maria (&quot;Canticles of Holy Mary&quot;) are 420 poems with musical notation, written in medieval Galician-Portuguese at the court of <b>Alfonso X of Castile</b>, called El Sabio, &quot;the Wise&quot; (1221-1284).</blockquote><a href="/songs/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria (Canticles of Holy Mary)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Mudéjar revolt of 1264-1266, James I of Aragon conquered Murcia on behalf of his son-in-law <b>Alfonso X of Castile</b>, ending the Third Taifa of Murcia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-murcia">Kingdom of Murcia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Toledo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.87,-4.03&amp;year=1261" aria-label="Toledo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alfonso X of Castile"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4735 4156h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4735 4156h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.87,-4.03&amp;year=1261">What was here in 1261? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
